**Q1.** The program will eventually print `inf`.

In IEEE 754 double precision, adding `1.0` to `x` repeatedly starting from `0.0` will eventually reach the largest finite representable value. The next addition of `1.0` will overflow, and according to the default rounding mode (round-to-nearest), the result becomes `math.inf`. At that point the condition `x < math.inf` is `False`, so the loop terminates and `print(x)` outputs `inf`.

---

**Q2.** The program will eventually print `inf`, and the result is the same as in Q1.

Starting from `1.0` and multiplying by `1.001` repeatedly causes exponential growth. After a finite number of iterations the value exceeds the largest finite double-precision number, and the multiplication overflows to `math.inf`. The loop condition `x < math.inf` then becomes `False`, the loop exits, and `print(x)` outputs `inf`.

---

**Q3.** Yes, there are inputs that lead to the output `"Done"`.

The loop terminates when `x` becomes `math.inf` (or `NaN`, since any comparison with `NaN` is `False`). This happens if the repeated addition of `inc` eventually overflows to infinity.

Two examples:
1. `inc = 1e308` — Starting from `x = 0.0`, the first addition `0.0 + 1e308` gives `1e308` (finite). The second addition `1e308 + 1e308 = 2e308` overflows to `inf`, so the loop exits and prints `"Done"`.
2. `inc = math.inf` — The first addition `0.0 + math.inf` immediately yields `math.inf`, so the condition `x < math.inf` is `False` on the very first check, and the program prints `"Done"`.

(Other inputs such as `inc = math.nan` also work, because `0.0 + nan` is `nan`, and `nan < math.inf` is `False`.) [end of text]


